 determined by the training data's maximal sampling rate, not the rendering resolution. An anti-aliasing strategy that combines a 3D smoothing filter with a bounding box expansion technique to limit high frequencies and prevent aliasing/dilation artifacts. It applies the 3D smoothing strategy to ensure each primitive is visible from at least one pixel of a training view. It identifies vertices on the bounding box used for tiling and shifts them outward, parallel to the screen, enlarging the footprint and increasing the minimum appearance size. This raises opacity along the primitive's extent, acting as a coarse band-limiting approximation. It cannot use MIP-Splatting's 2D Mip filter because LinPrim primitives do not rely on modifying the screen space Gaussian distribution. Adapts the kernel bandwidth to prevent aliasing when the volume is undersampled relative to the screen (low magnification or perspective). It calculates the Jacobian J of the local perspective distortion for each point and computes the ray-space ellipsoid G{JMV} using J to stretch the kernel appropriately. Swan '97 and Mueller '98 suggest linearly increasing splat kernel size and linearly decreasing splat amplitude. Mip-Splatting and Scaffold-GS employ conventional projection of 3D-GS, resulting in larger approximation error compared to methods with optimal projection strategies. The proposed optimal projection method can be seamlessly integrated with Mip-Splatting to reduce this projection error. Time and Memory Complexity: MIP-Splatting adds minimal overhead to standard 3DGS. The 3D smoothing filter is applied during training (once per primitive per m iterations) and becomes part of the static representation. The 2D Mip filter is applied during rendering, adding a simple covariance matrix addition (\Sigma{k2D} + sI) and a normalization factor, which is computationally inexpensive. Memory usage is identical to 3DGS as no additional primitives or data structures are required. Measured Performance: On the Blender dataset, MIP-Splatting achieves a PSNR of 32.81 at Full Res, 34.49 at 1/2 Res, 35.45 at 1/4 Res, and 35.50 at 1/8 Res, outperforming 3DGS and 3DGS+EWA. It significantly outperforms baselines in multi-scale testing scenarios. Strengths and Limitations: Strengths: Eliminates aliasing and dilation/erosion artifacts across various sampling rates (zoom in/out). Does not require multi-scale images for supervision, unlike Mip-NeRF. Tackles the reconstruction problem via inverse rendering, unlike EWA splatting which only considers rendering. Limitations: The 3D filter size is fixed based on training data, so it may not adapt to out-of-distribution scenes with significantly different sampling characteristics. The 2D Mip filter approximates a box filter but is still a Gaussian, which may cause slight oversmoothing compared to exact box filtering. When to Use: Use MIP-Splatting for Novel View Synthesis (NVS) in virtual reality, cinematography, and robotics, specifically when training and testing occur at different sampling rates or when zooming is expected. MIP-Splatting was introduced by Yu et al. in CVPR 2024. It addresses the limitations of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS), which suffers from aliasing and dilation artifacts when the sampling rate changes (e.g., focal length or camera distance variations). The method builds on the Nyquist-Shannon Sampling Theorem and the concept of pre-filtering in volume rendering. It replaces the empirical 2D dilation filter of 3DGS with a 2D Mip filter that mimics the physical box filter of a camera sensor. From Volume Rendering to 3D Gaussian Splatting: Theory and Applications, https://doi.org/10.1109/sibgrapi67909.2025.11223276, fetched 2026-10-26 Recent advances in 3D Gaussian splatting, https://doi.org/10.1007/s41095-024-0436-y, fetched 2026-10-26 Recent Advances and Trends in Learning‐Based 3D Representations, https://doi.org/10.1111/cgf.70530, fetched 2026-10-26 Gaussian Splatting — All Variants Cheat Sheet, https://www.