This method provides a data consistency mechanism for monocular depth estimation in radiance fields by leveraging geometric reprojection to identify reliable depth estimates and filter outliers. It solves the problem of noisy or incorrect depth predictions in sparse-view settings by verifying consistency across multiple camera viewpoints. It differs from Monocular Depth Consistency Loss by using reprojection error of image intensities to determine reliability rather than a closed-form least-squares scale/shift fit, and from Pseudo-depth Supervised Loss by explicitly filtering unreliable pixels via multi-view geometric checks. The method consists of two primary components: a Depth Consistency Check for outlier removal and a Depth Supervision Loss for training. Inputs: Depth maps Di, Dj for frames i and j. Camera poses and intrinsics for frames i and j. Image intensities for the nearest training view. Depth predictions from a main model (zm) and an augmented model (za). Outputs: A geometric consistency mask Md for outlier removal. A reliability mask ma (and mm) for loss weighting. The augmentation loss L{aug}. Data Structures: Depth maps: 2D arrays of float values. Masks: 2D boolean or binary arrays of same spatial dimensions as depth maps. Pixel coordinates \mathbf{u}i: 2D integer arrays representing grid positions. Step 1: Depth Outlier Removal (Consistency Check) This step filters noisy depth data by verifying geometric consistency between frames. 1. Unproject the depth map Di of frame i to 3D points using camera intrinsics. 2. Reproject these 3D points into the coordinate system of a nearby view j to obtain the projected depth D{i 	o j}. 3. Compare D{i 	o j} with the actual depth map Dj of view j. 4. Generate a mask Md where pixels are kept if the absolute difference is below a threshold \sigma. The formula for the mask is: Md = |D{i 	o j} - Dj| < \sigma where D{i 	o j} = Dj(\pij \pii^{-1}(\mathbf{u}i)), \mathbf{u}i are pixel coordinates of frame i, and \pi denotes projection functions. Step 2: Reliability Masking via Reprojection Error This step determines which depth estimate (main or augmented) is more reliable by comparing reprojection errors. 1. For each pixel, compute the Mean Squared Error (MSE) of intensities between a patch reprojected using zm and the corresponding patch in the nearest training view. Let this be em. 2. Repeat for za to get ea. 3. Define a threshold e	au. 4. Compute mask ma indicating if the augmented model is more reliable: ma =  egin{cases} 1 & 	ext{if } (ea \le em) \land (ea \le e	au) \ 0 & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} 5. Compute mask mm for the main model similarly (reliable if em \le ea and em \le e	au). Step 3: Depth Supervision Loss Calculation Compute the loss using the reliability masks and stop-gradient operators. L{aug} = \mathbb{1}\{ma=1\} \odot \| zm - \hat{
abla}(za) \|^2 + \mathbb{1}\{mm=1\} \odot \| \hat{
abla}(zm) - za \|^2 where \odot is element-wise product, \mathbb{1} is the indicator function, and \hat{
abla} is the stop-gradient operator. Parameters: \sigma = 0.2m: Empirical threshold for absolute relative error in depth reprojection. e	au: Threshold for reprojection MSE to filter incorrect depths. Value not given in sources. Pseudocode: Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def computedepthconsistencymask(Di, Dj, posei, posej, intrinsics, sigma=0.2): """ Computes geometric consistency mask between frame i and j. """ # Unproject Di to 3D points3di = unprojectdepthto3d(Di, posei, intrinsics) # Reproject to view j # Note: pij pii^-1(ui) logic handled by reprojection function Ditoj = reprojectdepth(points3di, posej, intrinsics) # Calculate absolute difference diff = abs(Ditoj - Dj) # Apply threshold Md = diff < sigma return Md def computereliabilitymasks(zm, za, nearestviewimage, patchsize, etau): """ Computes reliability masks based on reprojection intensity MSE. """ # Reproject patches using zm and za to nearest view # Assume function returns MSE per pixel em = computereprojectionmse(zm, nearestviewimage, patchsize) ea = computereprojectionmse(za, nearestviewimage, patchsize) # Mask for augmented model reliability ma = (ea <= em) & (ea <= etau) # Mask for main model reliability mm = (em <= ea) & (em <= etau) return ma, mm def computedepthsupervisionloss(zm, za, ma, mm): """ Computes the augmentation loss Laug. """ # Stop-gradient operator zmsg = stopgradient(zm) zasg = stopgradient(za) # Term 1: If augmented is reliable, supervise main with augmented (stop-grad) term1 = ma (zm - zasg)2 # Term 2: If main is reliable, supervise augmented with main (stop-grad) term2 = mm (za - zmsg)2 Laug = term1 + term2 return Laug Edge Cases and Pitfalls: Specular regions may cause intensity mismatches in reprojection, resulting in zero masks. This reduces supervision coverage but prevents incorrect depth supervision. If both models predict incorrect depth, both masks may be zero, providing no supervision for those pixels. Precision issues in unprojection/reprojection can affect Md accuracy near depth discontinuities. A specific formulation of the reliability-weighted loss where the main and augmented depth predictions supervise each other based on reprojection consistency. It sets apart from standard L1/L2 depth losses by dynamically masking unreliable pixels using multi-view intensity reprojection errors. Choose this when training radiance fields with sparse inputs where monocular depth priors are noisy. Link: Reliable Depth Estimation via Reprojection. Time Complexity: Dominated by reprojection operations. Unprojecting and reprojecting depth maps is O(N) where N is the number of pixels. Computing MSE over patches adds a constant factor based on patch size. Memory Complexity: Requires storing depth maps, masks, and intermediate 3D points. O(N) per frame. Measured Performance: Not given in the sources. Strengths: Effectively filters noisy depth data and specular artifacts. Improves reconstruction quality in sparse-view settings by ensuring only geometrically consistent depths are used for supervision. Limitations: Relies on the availability of nearby training views for reprojection. Fails in regions with no texture (specular/blank) where intensity MSE is unreliable. When to use: Sparse-view NeRF/Gaussian Splatting training where monocular depth estimates are used as regularizers. When not to use: Dense-view settings where depth consistency is trivial, or real-time applications where reprojection overhead is prohibitive. The method originates from research into regularizing sparse input radiance fields. The reliability masking via reprojection error was introduced in "Simple-RF" to handle sparse inputs. The depth consistency check for outlier removal was detailed in "EVolSplat" for efficient volume-based Gaussian Splatting in urban environments. Simple-RF: Regularizing Sparse Input Radiance Fields with Simpler Solutions, https://arxiv.org/abs/24